## Development Environment

This project uses [devbox](https://www.jetify.com/devbox) to manage the dev environment (`devbox shell`).

Canonical `devbox run` scripts (configured in `devbox.json`):

| Script | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `install` | `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` |
| `build` | `tsc -p tsconfig.build.json` to `dist/` (JavaScript, `.d.ts` declarations, source maps) |
| `test` | `vitest run --coverage` (thresholds: 80% lines/functions/statements) |
| `test-fast` | `vitest run` without coverage |
| `lint` | `eslint .` (typescript-eslint, type-aware) |
| `lint-fix` | `eslint . --fix` |
| `typecheck` | `tsc --noEmit` |
| `quality` | `lint` then `typecheck` — the gate CI runs |
| `audit` | `pnpm audit --prod --audit-level high` |
| `secrets-scan` | detect-secrets against `.secrets.baseline.json` |
| `security` | `audit` + `secrets-scan` |
| `secrets-baseline` | refresh `.secrets.baseline.json` with fixed flags (never run `detect-secrets scan` by hand) |
| `publish` | `publish vX.Y.Z [--dry-run]`: build, pack with the tag's version and publish (see Releasing the package) |
| `ci` | `quality` + `test` + `security`: the gates CI runs, then `ci-extra` when your repo defines it; run it before every push |

**Rule for every agent (human, CI, and AI): never call `pnpm`, `npm`, `npx`, `node`, `eslint`, `tsc`, `vitest` or `detect-secrets` directly. Always go through `devbox run <script>` (add a missing recipe to `devbox.json` rather than bypassing it).**

## Claude Code agents

Agents and slash commands come from the [`ika100/sdlc-foundry`](https://github.com/ika100/sdlc-foundry) marketplace, enabled in `.claude/settings.json`; each command's description says what it does. Read the platform's `docs/AGENTS.md` for the full orchestration model.

### Spec first

A feature starts with a spec, not code ([ADR-026](https://github.com/ika100/sdlc-foundry/blob/main/docs/adr/026-feature-specs.md)): `/component:spec <description>` writes `docs/specs/<NNN>-<slug>/spec.md` with acceptance criteria `AC-<NNN>.<n>` and asks you its open questions; approve it (`/component:spec approve <NNN>`), plan it (`/component:plan <NNN>`), then build it (`/component:build <NNN>`): acceptance tests are written from the criteria first and fail, the code makes them pass, a reviewer checks the result against the spec, and the PR lists every criterion. Tests that name a criterion are the spec in code: never weaken them; change the spec instead (`/component:spec --amend <NNN> <change>`). `/component:spec --all` shows where each spec stands; `devbox run spec-check` validates every spec and traces built ones to their tests (also the warn-only `specs` CI workflow). Small changes and bug fixes (`/component:fix`) need no spec, but stop when they would change a criterion.

## Feature Branch Workflow

Branch prefixes: `feature/`, `fix/`, `chore/`, `docs/`, `refactor/`, `release/`. Slugs: lowercase, hyphens, ≤40 chars. Main is protected; all changes via PR. Required CI checks: `quality`, `test`, `security`, `pr-title`. PR titles follow Conventional Commits (`type(scope): description ≤72 chars`).

## Where your additions go

Updates (`/platform:update`) overwrite the skeleton files below. Put your own work next to them, where an update keeps it:

| Updates overwrite | Your additions go in |
|---|---|
| the template's recipes in `devbox.json` (including `ci`) | gates: the `ci-extra` recipe (`devbox run ci` and the `ci-extra` CI check run it); other tools: recipes of your own in `devbox.json` (kept) |
| `.github/workflows/ci.yml` | a workflow file of your own in `.github/workflows/` |
| `.claude/settings.json` | your own permission entries (allow, ask, deny) in it; they are kept |
| `CLAUDE.md` | `.claude/project-notes.md` |
| other skeleton files (`scripts/publish.sh`, lint and test config ...) | nowhere: propose the change to the platform (`/issue:report`) |

## Conventions

- **Public API.** `src/index.ts` is the package's only entry point (`exports["."]` in `package.json`): everything a consumer may import is exported from there, documented with a TSDoc comment and tested. Anything not exported from it is internal and may change in any release.
- **ESM only.** `"type": "module"`, NodeNext resolution: relative imports end in `.js`. No CommonJS build.
- **TypeScript strict.** No `any` without a justifying comment; `devbox run typecheck` must be clean. The declarations in `dist/*.d.ts` are part of the API: a changed exported type is a changed API.
- **Versions follow semver** through Conventional Commits: `feat:` a minor release, `fix:` a patch, `feat!:` or `BREAKING CHANGE:` a major one. A breaking change of an export is a major release.
- **Dependencies.** Keep runtime `dependencies` few; types a consumer needs to use the API belong in `dependencies`, build and test tools in `devDependencies`.
- **Tests:** Vitest in `tests/` (`*.test.ts`), importing from `../src/index.js` the way a consumer would.
- **Docs:** feature specs in `docs/specs/` (index in `docs/backlog.md`), ADRs in `docs/adr/`.

## Releasing the package

`/component:release` tags `main`'s head `vX.Y.Z`. The tag's CI run repeats the gates, then its `publish` job runs `devbox run publish vX.Y.Z` ([ADR-125](https://github.com/ika100/sdlc-foundry/blob/main/docs/adr/125-library-releases-publish-to-github-on-the-tag.md)): it builds `dist/`, fails before publishing anything when the commit built is not the tagged one (or has changes), packs the package with the tag's version and publishes `@{{ github_org | lower }}/{{ project_name }}@X.Y.Z` to GitHub Packages (`https://npm.pkg.github.com`) with the job's `GITHUB_TOKEN` (`packages: write`). The tag is the version: `package.json` keeps its development version and the release writes no version file. A rerun of the same tag publishes nothing, because the registry already has that version. `devbox run publish vX.Y.Z --dry-run` builds, packs and checks locally without any network call.

Another repo installs a released version like this:

1. An `.npmrc` next to its `package.json`, committed (it holds no secret):

   ```ini
   @{{ github_org | lower }}:registry=https://npm.pkg.github.com
   //npm.pkg.github.com/:_authToken=${NODE_AUTH_TOKEN}
   ```

2. A token that can read packages, even for a public package: locally `export NODE_AUTH_TOKEN=$(gh auth token)` after `gh auth refresh -s read:packages`; in the consumer's CI the secret `PACKAGES_READ_TOKEN` (a token with `read:packages`), passed as `NODE_AUTH_TOKEN` to the install step (or `GITHUB_TOKEN` when this package grants the consuming repo access in its package settings).
3. `devbox run -- pnpm add @{{ github_org | lower }}/{{ project_name }}@X.Y.Z` in the consuming repo.

